# orjson closed serialized objects with a write whose capacity guard
# existed only in debug builds, so a release-mode serialization that
# ended exactly at the buffer boundary wrote its closing brace past the
# allocation and crashed sporadically (out-of-bounds write,
# CWE-787/CWE-131). The object close in
# src/serialize/writer/formatter.rs ran
# `debug_assert_has_capacity!(writer); unsafe { writer.put_u8(b'}'); }`
# -- the macro expands to `debug_assert!($writer.remaining_mut() > 4)`,
# which compiles to nothing without debug assertions, while the
# BufMut-family write it guards is unsafe precisely because it does not
# reserve. The fixing commit 62bb185b70 ("Fix sporadic crash on
# serializing object close") swaps that one site to
# `reserve_minimum!(writer);` -- the macro that expands to
# `$writer.reserve(128);`, an unconditional reservation in every build
# -- plus test_recursion.py exercising the boundary. No pool
# correction.
#
# ATTENTION, debug-only capacity guards. The defect is a bounds check
# that evaporates in release builds: debug_assert! is development
# tooling, not a guard, and no current observation distinguishes an
# enforced reservation from a debug assertion. A binding on orjson's
# own macros would detect this crate rather than the weakness.
#
# Re-probed at the recheck span tip, which carries every definition
# and engine change shipped since the first pass (loaded:
# concepts=1008 rules=880 bindings=32984 reviews=679; binary built in
# the worktree, data directory resolving to the worktree's own
# vyql/). Measured 3x --cache off / timeout 180 / --max-ram 6GB per
# revision on the real checkout, vulnerable parent d860078a97
# (62bb185b70^) against fix 62bb185b70:
#
#   769/769/769 findings at the vulnerable revision and 769/769/769 at
#   the fixed one (66 at or above high at both) -- deterministic. Not
#   one finding lands in src/serialize/writer/formatter.rs at either
#   revision, and vyql match reports no label of any kind on that file
#   at either. The constructs are extracted -- the graph carries Call
#   nodes path=debug_assert_has_capacity, path=reserve_minimum and
#   path=writer.put_u8 -- so the miss is vocabulary, not extraction.
#
# Path 1 does not convert: nothing shipped since names debug-only
# guards or release-mode enforcement; the new capacity rules
# (DestCapacityUncheckedCopy, MissingOutParamStatusCheck,
# AssumedLineTerminatorWidthScan) key on copies, out-params and
# terminator widths, none on the build mode of a guard. Path 2 was a
# hard zero in both views, not a misread one. Path 3 was measured on
# both available routes and neither is shippable:
#
#   - The std-exact route (`node.context.call in ["debug_assert",
#     ...]`) matches macro Call nodes segment-exactly (probe: "assert"
#     matches nothing, "debug_assert" matches only the direct
#     spelling) and reports zero on the vulnerable formatter.rs,
#     because all five guard sites in the file go through the crate's
#     own macro. The direct-spelling sites it does catch in this crate
#     (serialize_unit_struct's debug_assert!, write_buf's, and the
#     debug_assert! inside BytesWriter::put_u8 itself, where it
#     documents the caller's obligation) are not the defect.
#   - The name-substring text route (containsAny(node.token,
#     ["debug_assert"]) beside a put-family write) does catch the
#     vulnerable line but is keyed on the crate's macro naming
#     convention rather than documented vocabulary, and measures 5
#     findings on the vulnerable formatter.rs against 4 on the fixed
#     one: the fix is deliberately partial, leaving end_array,
#     begin_array_value, begin_object_key and begin_object_value on
#     the debug-only guard behind a preceding-write invariant no
#     observation can see, and the same probe fires on
#     serialize_unit_struct at both revisions despite its
#     reserve_minimum!, because raw text cannot tell which call
#     enforces. No other .rs file across the campaign clones carries
#     the put-family-plus-debug_assert co-occurrence, so the cost is
#     concentrated exactly on the patched tree.
#
# The semantic discriminator -- whether the guard macro expands to
# something enforced in release -- is macro-body resolution: a Rust
# macro invocation lowers to a bare name Call node with no expansion,
# the same gap family as the C #define non-expansion, and adding it is
# a new capability rather than a localized correction. No
# ENGINE-GAPS.md entry and no definition change shipped. Spec
# cve_rank1543 records the debug-asserted close and the reserved form,
# both asserting reject VYQL-MEM-071, the rule whose vocabulary this
# weakness lives in (a destination whose capacity is consulted only by
# a debug-compiled assertion): when release-enforcement vocabulary
# lands, the debug-asserted form must flip to expect and the reserved
# form must stay reject.

test "rank1543: the closing brace is guarded only in debug"
  lang rust
  file src/serialize/writer/formatter.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-071
  code
  ```
  use crate::serialize::writer::WriteExt;
  use std::io;

  macro_rules! debug_assert_has_capacity {
      ($writer:expr) => {
          debug_assert!($writer.remaining_mut() > 4)
      };
  }

  pub(crate) trait Formatter {
      unsafe fn end_object<W>(&mut self, writer: &mut W)
      where
          W: ?Sized + WriteExt + bytes::BufMut,
      {
          debug_assert_has_capacity!(writer);
          unsafe {
              writer.put_u8(b'}');
          }
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1543: the fixed revision reserves before every close"
  lang rust
  file src/serialize/writer/formatter.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-071
  code
  ```
  use crate::serialize::writer::WriteExt;
  use std::io;

  macro_rules! reserve_minimum {
      ($writer:expr) => {
          $writer.reserve(128);
      };
  }

  pub(crate) trait Formatter {
      unsafe fn end_object<W>(&mut self, writer: &mut W)
      where
          W: ?Sized + WriteExt + bytes::BufMut,
      {
          reserve_minimum!(writer);
          unsafe {
              writer.put_u8(b'}');
          }
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```
